<p>^ Apparently, identifying that SCP-2938 is [spoiler] triggers the effect, even via indirect knowledge. They would not be aware of this however, because it is not an unexpected result that the substance cannot be identified.<br />
Mini-philosophy lesson.<br />
There are five orders of ignorance:<br />
0th: Lack of Ignorance: I completely comprehend X.<br />
1st: Order Ignorance: I don't know these specific aspects of X<br />
2nd Order Ignorance:I know there are things I don't know about X, but I don't know what they are<br />
3rd Order Ignorance: I know that I don't know whether I know everything about X or not.<br />
4th Order Ignorance: I am unaware that I don't know everything about X</p>
<p>The cogitohazard seems to trigger at 2nd order, and prevent anyone from progressing <em>beyond</em> 2nd order. They know there is something they don't know, and have correctly identified that there is a cogitohazard that prevents them from moving beyond that, but the nature of the cogitohazard prevents them from identifying SCP-2938 beyond these traits. You can establish tests to identify SCP-2938, but you cannot comprehend the results. Even blind shotgunning attempts with standard testing batteries are foiled by the fact that knowing enough to identify SCP-2938 as [spoiler] apparently triggers the cogitohazard.</p>

