<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>What's this got to do with H2O?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Everything.</p>
<p>If you pour water into a different container you will have briefly two distinct masses of water, which will combine (you can see this in time lapse photos of pouring; or just pour a little bit of water into your glass and then add some more later).</p>
<p>At 50° water is in a liquid state, and it contains water (which under IUPAC conventions would be named oxidane).</p>
<p>At 0° water is a solid (which we call &quot;ice&quot; in English) that is a crystal made of water (which if you name it as an acid is hydroxylic acid).</p>
